WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.000
Agent Kister was running for his life. Every&nbsp;
breath had burned in his throat, like inhaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.240
chlorine gas. He sped through the dark, surrounded&nbsp;
by deafening metallic screams that bounced off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.920
eternity of pipes scaling and strangling the&nbsp;
walls around him. As far as he was concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.960
this was what Hell looked like. A nightmare&nbsp;
boiler room that would somehow be less scary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.920
if Freddy Kreuger was there, because at least&nbsp;
there’d be some kind of recognizably human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:35.040
intelligence to reason with. The Pipes&nbsp;
- SCP-015 - are truly unknowable.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.920
As an SCP Foundation Mobile Task Force&nbsp;
member, Agent Kister had seen some truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.680
horrific things in the line of duty. He’d been&nbsp;
there during SCP-682 containment breaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.560
firing an assault rifle at the beast as its&nbsp;
scales hardened into a bulletproof carapace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.240
He’d seen the femur breaker used on some poor,&nbsp;
godforsaken D-Class to lure Uncle Larry back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.200
the containment chamber for some midnight fun.&nbsp;
In the most anxiety-inducing mission of his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.640
he’d once even bagged 096’s weeping face&nbsp;
after it slaughtered a whole mountain village.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.880
And these damn pipes were going&nbsp;
to be the thing that killed him.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.800
He turned a sharp corner, trying to&nbsp;
block out the screams of his teammates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.920
Four of them had been sent in - The rest were&nbsp;
already dead, or worse. Stupid, stupid, stupid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.440
They’d made a classic military mistake:&nbsp;
Underestimating the capabilities of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.600
enemy. And in Agent Kister’s experience,&nbsp;
this had always been a capital offense.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.120
All to install that machine. That stupid&nbsp;
little remote control reconnaissance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.400
vehicle. That’s what they’d given their lives for…

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.640
Agent Kister tried to blink away the&nbsp;
memory of Agent Montgomery’s face.&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.760
Monty was the youngest member of the team -&nbsp;
28 years old, his first major mission into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.240
the heart of a skip. Kister wanted to&nbsp;
remember him how he’d first seen him:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.680
A bright-eyed greenhorn, ready to protect the&nbsp;
human race from the horrors that lay in the dark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.832
But he’d been the first to go&nbsp;
when everything got F.U.B.A.R.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.240
It was the smaller pipes that got Monty. Four&nbsp;
of them shot out, spearing his lungs and heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.520
pinning him in place. Even if they tried to cut&nbsp;
him out, he would have been a goner. He coughed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.680
up blood and went ghost-white faster than you’d&nbsp;
ever imagine. Kister would never forget, for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.440
little time he had left to live, the horrible&nbsp;
sight of the light leaving that kid’s eyes.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.760
And all this because one of them had&nbsp;
tripped on one of the pipes on the way out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.840
and busted it. The mission was already&nbsp;
done. What an awful, pointless way to die.&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.920
Kister was yanked from his aching&nbsp;
thoughts by a sudden obstacle in the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.640
A huge, towering pipe that looked like&nbsp;
a pillar of flesh, covered in bulbous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.240
staring eyes. The thought crossed his mind, Jesus,&nbsp;
these pipes can really be made out of anything…

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.120
He looked down and saw a series of rubber pipes&nbsp;
slithering towards his feet along the ground like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.880
snakes. It was time to run again, but how long&nbsp;
could you even run for when the very location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.280
around you wants you dead? They should’ve&nbsp;
sent the Mole Rats ]for a job like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.680
The infinitely reconfiguring pipes changed&nbsp;
the layout of the warehouse around him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.640
making escape seem all the more unfeasible.&nbsp;
That’s when he started thinking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:24.640
terrible thing that’d happened to Agent Greene…
When 015 turned hostile, they needed to make a run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.560
for it, fast. It wasn’t unlike being a pathogen&nbsp;
inside a living body - You start to make trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:35.440
and the body’s immune system is going to come&nbsp;
for you. And one of the most common mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:42.000
for a body fighting a virus is a fever - A sudden&nbsp;
increase in temperature that burns out the threat.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.760
The group turned and ran as the&nbsp;
pipes started twisting towards them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.760
Agent Greene panicked and began striking&nbsp;
at the pipes around him with his hands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.360
The first instinct would have been to shoot,&nbsp;
but no guns were permitted inside the SCP-015&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.840
warehouse. It reacted severely to any kind&nbsp;
of tool being brought into its proximity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.600
Several dead agents and researchers could tell&nbsp;
you all about this if they hadn’t made that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:11.440
same mistake. But when things were already&nbsp;
going sideways, it left them defenseless.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.600
Agent Greene was soon boxed in, caged by&nbsp;
a latticework of pipes in varying sizes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.240
He tried desperately to push them out of the way&nbsp;
and make an escape route, but the hissing noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.080
was getting louder and louder as the pipes&nbsp;
started to turn a glowing red around him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.240
After all, when a body needs to deal with a&nbsp;
hostile foreign object, it burns out the threat.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.320
Kister wished he could shake the awful,&nbsp;
high-pitched wail that Agent Greene made when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.080
the heat inside his pipe cage became unbearable.&nbsp;
It was like seeing a human get caught inside a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.640
giant bug zapper. His skin went black and charred,&nbsp;
and soon after, the heat had risen to such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:56.160
insane degree that Agent Greene was reduced&nbsp;
to little more than ash in his pipe furnace.&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.680
But Kister couldn’t afford to dwell. He kept&nbsp;
running, vaulting over a long, ragged pipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:08.000
made from stinking human hair. This terrible&nbsp;
place stank of motor oil, mold, and death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.120
But maybe those perceptions had been colored&nbsp;
by the terrible things he’d seen here.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.000
There it was again. That awful choked&nbsp;
screaming coming from inside the pipes.&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.440
Agent Boggs. It was a nightmare to hear him shriek&nbsp;
like that. Boggs had been one of the toughest men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.320
that Kister had ever met - He mentored him,&nbsp;
back when he first became an MTF Member.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.440
Having Boggs on the team meant experience.&nbsp;
It meant safety. It meant that things would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.880
go okay. Kister had seen Boggs stare down some&nbsp;
of the most horrifying skips imaginable and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.520
even flinch. And yet, here he was, screaming&nbsp;
and bawling like a hurt child in the pipes.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.680
Boggs was the next one to get taken.&nbsp;
As he and Kister ran through the dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.040
trying to find a way out, they’d passed a&nbsp;
huge pipe made of a soft, foam-like substance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.840
A seam in the side of the pipe had yawned&nbsp;
open, and a tangle of writhing tentacles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:05.280
each one barbed with thorns like the stem of a&nbsp;
rosebush. They enveloped Agent Boggs, cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:12.240
into his weathered skin. He gasped in pain, unable&nbsp;
to scream. It happened so fast. Before Kister even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.280
had a chance to grab him, he was pulled into&nbsp;
the pipe and the crevice sealed behind him.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.600
And that was when the screaming&nbsp;
really started for poor Agent Boggs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.840
Kister hoped, for his sake,&nbsp;
that he would die sometime soon.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.720
Shrouded in the fog of his dark thoughts,&nbsp;
Agent Kister turned a corner and felt cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.720
hard metal suddenly collide with&nbsp;
his face, breaking his nose with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.160
unpleasant crunch. He’d been clotheslined&nbsp;
by a low pipe that hadn’t been there before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:46.000
and now, he was laid out on his back, face&nbsp;
humming with pain. Thoughts and feelings swam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.720
He looked up into the seemingly infinite web&nbsp;
of pipes weaving through the air above him. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:57.120
was right earlier, he just knew it. The place&nbsp;
was Hell. It’d be his eternal resting place.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.640
Suddenly, a new, huge shape moved into&nbsp;
the space above him. A pipe of ancient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.680
rusty iron that looked older than even&nbsp;
the warehouse holding this whole mess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:13.360
Right above him, there was a huge valve fixed&nbsp;
into the pipe’s belly. He watched, helpless, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.520
the valve opened itself with a squeal, and a huge,&nbsp;
dark mass came pouring out of it onto his body.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:27.120
A sudden weight. A sudden warmth. Sound and&nbsp;
movement and tiny scratching claws. Rats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.720
Thousands of big, hungry, black rats with&nbsp;
gnarled yellow teeth, clumps of fur purged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:39.040
from their skin by mange. Big, ropey, worm-like&nbsp;
tails swaying and whipping at the air. Who knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:43.760
how long they’d been in that pipe? Who knows&nbsp;
when these rats had eaten anything that wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:50.480
their brothers and sisters? And here was Agent&nbsp;
Kister, given unto them by the glorious pipes.&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:56.320
The last thing he ever saw were those nasty&nbsp;
yellow teeth, because they ate his eyes first.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:01.360
Word of the four disappeared - and presumed&nbsp;
dead - operatives soon reached Dr. Charles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:08.240
Ogden Gears, the legendarily taciturn senior&nbsp;
researcher that managed the SCP-015 project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.080
among a myriad of others.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:16.640
His face didn’t betray a flicker of emotion when&nbsp;
he was informed about the men lost to SCP-015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.200
It wasn’t that he didn’t feel for them,&nbsp;
more that he deemed it both unproductive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.160
and unprofessional to dwell on the deaths&nbsp;
of personnel when carrying out their duties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.960
After all, being killed by an anomaly&nbsp;
was simply an occupational hazard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:36.080
and a generous stipend would be sent to their&nbsp;
families to compensate for their tragic loss.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.400
Dr. Gears simply asked, “Was the mission&nbsp;
successful, in spite of the casualties?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.400
Did the team manage to install the&nbsp;
Modular Robotic Vehicle at the epicenter?”

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.400
He was told that the MRV had been&nbsp;
successfully installed, at least.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.720
Dr. Gears nodded and began scheduling&nbsp;
a second exploratory mission for a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.840
months in the future to check&nbsp;
the status of the machine.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:02.640
Still, in the meantime, the MRV would chug&nbsp;
along, doing what humans couldn’t - or perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:08.240
shouldn’t - do within the nightmarish mess&nbsp;
of ever-growing pipes. It would roll through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.920
collecting information on everything around it,&nbsp;
hopefully giving the SCP Foundation an inroad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:19.680
to mapping the whole thing without needing to&nbsp;
send a cavalcade of staff into the danger zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.600
just to discover more about these peculiar pipes.&nbsp;
Sure, some lives were lost getting it in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.600
but this measure would likely save&nbsp;
even more lives in the long run.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:35.280
That was… Until something went wrong.&nbsp;
The exact nature of what had happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:41.680
to the MRV inside the domain of the pipes was a&nbsp;
mystery, but it wasn’t doing its intended job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:47.520
and the SCP Foundation needed to find out why.&nbsp;
The second exploratory mission was required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.680
much sooner than they’d initially anticipated,&nbsp;
with a dual purpose: Collecting the readings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:58.320
and finding out what exactly had&nbsp;
happened to the MRV in the first place.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:03.680
He included a more specific set of instructions&nbsp;
for the next mission: Only three members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:10.960
personnel to enter SCP-015 this time, to minimize&nbsp;
potential loss. One trained technician to collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:16.480
and read the diagnostics, and two guards to&nbsp;
maintain discipline and safety under pressure.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.320
With a setup like that, what&nbsp;
could possibly go wrong?

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.880
Dr. Gears’ various assistants began&nbsp;
headhunting the perfect team for the job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.600
Junior Researcher Lon, wanting to prove&nbsp;
herself and climb up the ranks, put her name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:35.040
forward. She was young, intelligent, and&nbsp;
had the initiative to get the job done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.920
Though there was one thing that she hadn’t&nbsp;
disclosed when she was applying for this important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:47.520
new position: Junior Researcher Lon suffered&nbsp;
from claustrophobia, the fear of enclosed spaces.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.920
Then the hunt was on for the two members of&nbsp;
Foundation military personnel who’d escort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.880
Lon to the MRV to take the readings.&nbsp;
Like the two priests in The Exorcist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.000
they decided to recruit one grizzled,&nbsp;
experienced operative and one extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:06.640
cautious young buck straight out of&nbsp;
the Foundation MTF Training Academy.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:12.880
The younger of the two operatives, codenamed Agent&nbsp;
Two, was careful and conscientious to a fault.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:18.240
He did things by the book or not at all.&nbsp;
The older operative, codenamed Agent Six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:23.040
was quite the opposite. He’d done this job&nbsp;
long enough to operate solely on instinct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.760
and resented his employers for expecting&nbsp;
him to approach his missions any other way.&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:34.080
Lon, Agent Two, and Agent Six. The team&nbsp;
was assembled, and when the time came,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:40.000
they were taken to SCP-015 to execute their&nbsp;
mission. It should have been simple enough:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:46.400
Get in, collect the readings, and get back out.&nbsp;
Though a few weeks earlier, the same thing would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.960
have been said for the mission to install&nbsp;
the MRV which left for Mobile Task Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:58.720
operatives dead or worse. Nothing was ever as&nbsp;
simple as it seemed when it came to SCP-015.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:03.520
They were only allowed flashlights inside.&nbsp;
Anything else carried a substantial risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:09.440
of activating SCP-015’s defensive state,&nbsp;
putting all of their lives in jeopardy.

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:15.920
Lon had tried to keep things light, masking her&nbsp;
own terrible fear at this strange new situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.200
She’d joked that perhaps the three&nbsp;
of them should get Mario hats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.120
seeing as they were plumbers now.&nbsp;
Agent Two had happily laughed along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:27.600
suggesting that perhaps he should be&nbsp;
Luigi. Agent Six was utterly unamused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:32.960
by this whole situation and wanted no part of&nbsp;
it, which is honestly just so Wario of him.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:37.760
They followed along a carefully-mapped route&nbsp;
through the pipes, being careful not to touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:44.480
any of them. After all, you never know what could&nbsp;
set SCP-015 off. Agent Six didn’t share in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:50.400
merriment or their concern. In fact, he had&nbsp;
contempt for them and this whole situation.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:55.120
He thought Lon and Agent Two were a pair of&nbsp;
frightened, skittish children willing to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:00.720
whatever Foundation-assigned bullhonkey they&nbsp;
were given. He didn’t believe that SCP-015 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:06.480
anywhere near as dangerous as they’d been told.&nbsp;
In all likelihood, knowing the eggheads up top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:11.040
they just didn’t want men of action like him&nbsp;
breaking any of their precious little toys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:16.960
while conducting missions on their behalf.&nbsp;
This whole thing was a big, stupid joke.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:22.960
As they ducked and weaved through the confines&nbsp;
of SCP-015, the tension between them all just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:29.280
silently grew. Their flashlights played along the&nbsp;
pipes, revealing the insane variety of shapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:36.080
sizes, compositions, and materials&nbsp;
in here. Wood, steel, flesh, bone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.520
glass, pressed ash, granite, and so&nbsp;
much more. And yet, there were no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:47.680
pipes comprised of any standard pipe-making&nbsp;
material, like lead, PVC plastic, or copper.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:52.400
It was undeniably freaky, but when&nbsp;
you work with SCPs for long enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:58.720
you learn to stop asking questions about the more&nbsp;
minor forms of strangeness inherent to anomalies.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.880
The composition only got narrower as&nbsp;
they delved deeper into the warehouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:07.280
Lon felt the anxiety like a hand&nbsp;
gripping her throat and squeezing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:11.600
but tried still to keep it hidden.&nbsp;
Agent Six, the largest among them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:17.440
almost got stuck a few times, trying to crawl&nbsp;
into spaces too tight for him to fit. He felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:22.400
like murdering Lon when she threw out a casual&nbsp;
comparison to Winnie the Pooh in the process.&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:28.560
When they reached the MRV, it gave them an ounce&nbsp;
of momentary relief. The journey was halfway done.

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:33.360
But that comfort was quickly squashed by&nbsp;
seeing what had actually happened to the MRV:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.560
It had been speared by a pipe, and new&nbsp;
pipes had grown through and out of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:44.720
effectively rooting it in place. This caused&nbsp;
immediate debate among the group around one key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:49.280
issue: Did this mean that the MRV was&nbsp;
now technically part of the pipes? And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:54.080
by extension, would the pipes feel the need&nbsp;
to defend themselves if the databanks were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:59.920
removed from the MRV in order to deliver the&nbsp;
readings back to the researchers at Site-17?

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:05.120
While Lon and Agent Two carefully discussed the&nbsp;
issue, Agent Six lost his temper, and decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:09.760
to show these two foolish kids how it was done.&nbsp;
He claimed that the two of them were worrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:15.120
about a bunch of Foundation hooey that they were&nbsp;
inexperienced and gullible enough to believe:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:18.560
As if this bunch of weird&nbsp;
pipes was actually dangerous!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:23.280
It was like how every six anomalies there’s&nbsp;
apparently one capable of ending the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.960
It’s all a bunch of overblown&nbsp;
fearmongering nonsense!

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:31.680
And to prove that he wasn’t just all talk&nbsp;
on this front, Agent Six pried open the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:36.880
of the MRV and slid out the data cards - which&nbsp;
seemed to be covered in some kind of viscous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:43.120
unpleasant liquid from the pipes that’d penetrated&nbsp;
the device. Still, he’d gotten what they came for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.720
He could have done this whole thing&nbsp;
alone, without this pair of bedwetters.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:50.160
He scoffed and said, “Kids. I&nbsp;
don’t know how you two survive.”

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:56.080
But he shouldn’t have spoken so soon. The&nbsp;
large pipe that he didn’t even realize he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:00.720
was standing on opened up beneath his feet,&nbsp;
and seconds later, he was immersed up to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:05.440
shoulders in it. Six began to let out the&nbsp;
most horrifying shrieks you’ve ever heard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:10.000
and when Agent Two and Lon ran over to&nbsp;
try to help him out, they realized why.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:14.320
The pipe below Agent Six was&nbsp;
filled with flowing molten iron.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:19.760
By the time they pulled Agent Six out - Well,&nbsp;
Agent Six from the shoulders up, considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:25.520
that was all that was left - he was already dead.&nbsp;
And despite the actual aggressor being destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:32.880
SCP-015 wasn’t done. It was ready to clean&nbsp;
house. As Agent Two and Lon attempted to flee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:37.920
some eye-level pipes around them burst,&nbsp;
firing crystalized glass into their faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:42.000
cutting into their skin and&nbsp;
eyes. But they needed to push on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.520
despite the pain if they&nbsp;
wanted to survive and get out.

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:50.880
The two of them ran, horrifying noises of&nbsp;
twisting and bursting pipes roaring behind them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:56.960
Pipes made of thorns and bones started to grow,&nbsp;
blocking off their exits until they were trapped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.960 --> 00:17:01.120
Lon, utilizing her small frame, was able&nbsp;
to crawl through a gap in the pipes into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:06.240
adjoining chamber. Two was given a horrifying&nbsp;
surprise when a pipe burst near his hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:11.680
showering his flesh in thick, corrosive&nbsp;
gunk. His hand melted away at the wrist.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:15.600
As Lon called for help in the tiny,&nbsp;
cramped chamber she was locked in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:20.400
Two ran off to try and find an exit, where&nbsp;
he could bring more people in to help them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:25.520
But it would already be too late for poor&nbsp;
Lon. A thick, honey-like substance began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:31.200
bleeding into the chamber, disgusting in its&nbsp;
overpowering saccharine flavor and gloopy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:36.480
viscous thickness. The chamber seemed to&nbsp;
get smaller and smaller as Lon screamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:41.760
and the honey level rose. Eventually, it got&nbsp;
higher than her mouth and nose could reach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:48.720
She gave a horrifying final gurgle, and then she&nbsp;
was gone, entombed forever between the pipes.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:53.600
Agent Two kept running. His flashlight&nbsp;
was beginning to die, but he didn’t care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:57.920
As his eyes bled from the splinters of glass&nbsp;
and what was left of his hand dripped from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:03.600
jagged nub of his wrist, all he could do was&nbsp;
keep moving forwards. He panted and screamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:08.400
and stumbled in the dark until he tripped on&nbsp;
something he didn’t notice and pitched forwards.

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:15.680
He tumbled into the gaping maw of a nearby&nbsp;
pipe, surrounded by unseeable, unknowable ooze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:21.440
as he tumbled further down into the seemingly&nbsp;
infinite darkness. It was too dark to see and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:28.000
too cramped to move anywhere but straight down.&nbsp;
All he could do was scream and scream and scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:34.080
until his voice was hoarse and his throat bled,&nbsp;
though nobody was there to even hear his cries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:38.320
Days later, when his skin started&nbsp;
to shred off, it was almost welcome.

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:41.360
Dr. Gears would later receive the&nbsp;
news of this tragically failed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:45.040
mission and the three operatives&nbsp;
now declared MIA because of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:49.440
He sighed, and wrote up his report on&nbsp;
the matter, closing with the sentence:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:56.560
“Data deemed non-vital in light of lost staff.&nbsp;
SCP-015 classification level review suggested.”

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:01.280
Now go check out “SCP-049 - Boiled,&nbsp;
Scrambled and Fried - Plague Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:04.880
Tale” and “What Actually Happened&nbsp;
to Major Alexei Belitrov?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:11.600
SCP-2273 - Tale” for more chilling tales&nbsp;
from the files of the SCP Foundation!

